useful_life_text:
We record property and equipment at historical cost less accumulated depreciation and amortization, which is recognized using the straight-line
method over the following estimated useful lives: (i) buildings and improvements – up to 40 years; (ii) restaurant equipment – up to 18 years; (iii) furniture, fixtures and other – up to 10 years;
(iv) manufacturing equipment – up to 30 years; and (v) capital leases – up to 40 years or lease term.

Premises and equipment are stated at cost less accumulated depreciation
and amortization. Estimated useful lives are generally as follows: buildings—39 years; furniture and fixtures—7 years;
computer and communications equipment—3 to 9 years; power plants—15 years; tugs and barges—15 years; and terminals, pipelines and equipment—3 to 25 years. Estimated useful lives for software costs are generally 3 to 5 years.
